  "query_preview": "..\n(Wednesday night through next Tuesday)\nIssued at 1247 PM CDT Tue Oct 27 2026\n\nMain weather maker will arrive starting late Wednesday night into\nFriday morning across the area. The abundant low level moisture\nfrom the Gulf will combine with the moisture crossing Mexico from\nTS Vance and a developing upper level trough over the West Coast\nto produce an area of showers and storms across the area. Models\nstill have their differences, even in the higher res CAM's just\nnow starting to come in, but a large area of rainfall across much\nof the western half of Texas looks on target. The model\ndifferences are mainly in timing (some models focus the rain\nacross the area Thursday afternoon and evening, while others are\nmore Thursday night into Friday morning) and where the heavier\nrain axis will develop. PWAT values climb to near 1.5 inches, 2 to\n3 standard deviations higher than normal, so these showers and\nstorms will be efficient. Question is really of location. Does the\nmost widespread heaviest rainfall end up over West Central Texas\nfrom the Concho Valley south into the Edwards Plateau, or a little\nfarther west into the Permian Basin and Trans Pecos? Right now, \nit looks like just west of the area. This still means fairly\nwidespread rain, heavy at times, for areas along/west of a\nSonora-San Angelo-Sweetwater line with much lighter totals east\nacross the Heartland and Hill Country. Heaviest totals though may\nbe across the eastern Permian Basin. \n\nOne of those cases where the training largely south-north moving\nconvection may have a fairly sharp boundary between 0.5-1 inch\ntotal and heavier totals of 2-3+ inches. Hopefully, models will\ncome into a little better agreement as we go another 24 hours and\nwhere this sharp cut off occurs across West Central Texas.\n\nA bit of a lull in the systems for Halloween and Sunday where\nconditions look mainly dry and rain chances diminish, before the\nnext upper level trough sweeps into the area for Sunday night into\nMonday. Rain chances increase once again, although the potential\nfor heavier rainfall looks smaller.\n\nFinally, as for temperatures nothing terribly remarkable for this\ntime of year, with highs in the 70s and lower 80s. Coolest\nreadings will come after the early next week trough axis passes\nand drier air can work its way in. Some lows in the 40s possible\nbut may be the middle of next week before that happens.",
